tions":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"Let \\(C\\) denote the set of computable real numbers. Define\n\\[\nf:C\\cap(0,1)\\to C,\\qquad f(x)=\\tan\\!\\left(\\pi\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)\\right).\n\\]\nThe angle lies in \\((-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)\\), where tangent is a bijection onto \\(\\mathbb R\\). Since \\(\\pi\\) is computable and tangent preserves computability, \\(f\\) maps computable inputs to computable outputs.\n\nIts inverse is\n\\[\nf^{-1}(y)=\\tfrac12+\\frac{\\arctan y}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nBy the assumed inverse preservation of computability, this is computable for every \\(y\\in C\\), and it lies strictly between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\). Thus \\(f\\) is a bijection between the two sets.\n\nFinal answer: Yes, \\(\\lvert C\\cap(0,1)\\rvert=\\lvert C\\rvert=\\aleph_0\\)."}],"phase":"final_answer","role":"assistant"}],"parallel_tool_calls":true,"presence_penalty":0,"previous_response_id":null,"prompt_cache_key":null,"prompt_cache_retention":"24h","reasoning":{"context":"all_turns","effort":"medium","mode":"standard","summary":null},"safety_identifier":null,"service_tier":"default","store":false,"temperature":1,"text":{"format":{"type":"text"},"verbosity":"medium"},"tool_choice":"auto","tool_usage":{"image_gen":{"input_tokens":0,"input_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"output_tokens":0,"output_tokens_details":{"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":0},"total_tokens":0},"web_search":{"num_requests":0}},"tools":[],"top_logprobs":0,"top_p":0.98,"truncation":"disabled","usage":{"input_tokens":128,"input_tokens_details":{"cache_write_tokens":0,"cached_tokens":0},"output_tokens":383,"output_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":152},"total_tokens":511},"user":null,"metadata":{}}
